WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.430
- [Narrator] This is the
incredible true story of

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.490
how one person sold millions
of millions of dollars

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.250
worth of paintings to the art world.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:13.250
But what no one knew is that
the paintings were all fake.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.400
(pensive music)

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.360
The story begins in the year 1995.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.670
An art collector in Germany takes a look

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.540
at a painting that he
acquired a decade ago.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.360
The piece is called "Erigone and Mara."

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.150
It was supposedly done
by Johannes Molzahn,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.300
a fairly well-known German
expressionist painter.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.210
And for some reason, the
collector begins to have doubts

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.670
whether it's actually genuine.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.010
And so he decides to get confirmation

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.910
and he submits the painting
for scientific analysis.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.670
Not long after, he receives the results

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:50.940
and his concern is validated.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.663
The painting is fake.

00:00:52.663 --> 00:00:54.330
(sharp music)

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.340
Feeling anger that he was deceived,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.820
the collector begins tracking
down other Molzahn paintings

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.340
that were sold around the same time.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.460
One that was purchased by Molzahn's widow

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.810
and another one bought by
a Berlin gallery owner.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.810
Both of them also turn out to be fakes.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.330
And the final nail in
the coffin comes from

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.110
a Molzahn that was said to
have been painted in 1920,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.660
but experts discovered that it uses

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:26.660
a color pigment that wasn't
even available until 1957,

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:31.899
37 years later than the
painting's supposed origin date.

00:01:31.899 --> 00:01:33.540
(pensive music)

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.600
With all this evidence, the
collector goes to the police

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.020
and he tells them all the
information he has uncovered.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.270
An official investigation begins

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.180
in order to find whoever
is forging these paintings,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.390
and this investigation
seemed to make quick progress

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:51.630
as police discovered that
all these fake paintings

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.380
were sold by one single
art dealer from Berlin.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.082
So naturally, police question
him to find out his source.

00:01:59.082 --> 00:02:00.840
(pensive music continues)

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.910
The art dealer then tries to point them

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.770
to the next link in the chain,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.070
the person he bought
the fake paintings from.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.170
But despite their best efforts,

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:14.160
the police are unable to find this source.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.010
And even if they could, it wouldn't matter

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.530
as they discovered that
the statute of limitations

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.080
has run out on this crime.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.790
Too many years had passed

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.600
and whoever forged these
paintings just got away.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.750
But what the police don't know
is that these fake paintings

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.590
only represented the
very tip of the iceberg.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:37.290
In fact, they were hot on
the trail of uncovering

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.550
one of the largest art fraud
scandals in modern history,

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.800
and the man at the center of it all

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.071
has just slipped through their fingers.

00:02:47.071 --> 00:02:48.540
(bright music)

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.230
While the police office
investigating this crime in Berlin

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.470
closes the case on these
fake Molzahn paintings,

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.860
about 300 miles away in Viersen, Germany,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.810
a man sits in his studio

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.083
ready to begin creating a new masterpiece.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.920
His name is Wolfgang Beltracchi

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.230
and he's currently putting
the finishing touches

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.930
on a painting he calls
"Madchen mit Schwan,"

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.910
Girl with Swan.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:18.090
The canvas he's working
on is a genuine old canvas

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.100
he had found at a flea market,

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.270
but what he fills this blank
canvas with is not genuine.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.550
When he's ready to sign his masterpiece,

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.050
instead of putting his own name,

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.260
he signs the canvas as
Heinrich Campendonk,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.000
another German expressionist

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.162
from the same era as Johannes Molzahn.

00:03:36.162 --> 00:03:37.590
(bright music)

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:38.820
With his job done,

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.730
Wolfgang calls in his
wife Helena into the room,

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.910
who prepares herself for their next step.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:45.960
She takes the painting

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.660
to an art dealer who works for Christie's,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.833
a world-renowned auction
house based in London.

00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:53.160
(bright music continues)

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.180
In the art world,

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.970
Christie's and Sotheby's
are the two places

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:00.630
for the most expensive and
desired artworks in the world,

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.570
often auctioning off
paintings worth millions

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:06.480
or even hundreds of millions.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.350
When looking at the fake
painting, the dealer is impressed.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.170
It certainly looks of authentic,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:14.790
but where did it come from

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.310
and how did Helena come
to be in possession

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:18.903
of such a rare find?

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:21.000
(pensive music)

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.370
Helena then makes up her story.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.470
She claims that the painting originates

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.660
from a man called Alfred Flechtheim,

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.170
an art dealer who lived
through the Second World War.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.930
Alfred Flechtheim is not made up.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:36.390
He was in fact a real person,

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.940
but Helena had no real connection to him.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:40.950
(pensive music continues)

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.680
After the Nazis rose to power in Germany,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.730
they began confiscating art
which they deemed degenerate.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.340
They were particularly
focused on modern art

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.390
as it represented something
the Nazis didn't want.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.850
It was known to break conventional rules

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.220
and challenge traditions,

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:01.380
and the Nazis especially cracked down

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.753
on Jewish artists and collectors.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.830
Alfred Flechtheim was
a prominent art dealer,

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.720
and Flechtheim was Jewish,

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.170
so the Nazis seized
several galleries he owned

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:16.260
and took away his art collection.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.140
Flechtheim eventually
had to flee from Germany,

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.190
and it is unknown

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.290
just how many paintings
from his collection

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.810
were lost or destroyed.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:28.080
This part about Alfred
Flechtheim is all true

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.780
and it is well-documented history.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.896
But now, the lies begin.

00:05:32.896 --> 00:05:34.080
(pensive music)

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.580
Because according to Helena,

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.040
before all of his
galleries could be ceased,

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.020
Flechtheim stashed many paintings

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.810
with her grandfather, Werner Jager,

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.000
who was able to hide the collection

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.950
from the plundering Nazis.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.110
And now, many decades later,

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:51.900
her grandfather has
passed his collection down

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.189
to Helena and her sister Jeanette.

00:05:54.189 --> 00:05:55.260
(pensive music continues)

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.810
The Christie's dealer is
fascinated with the tale

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.480
and he eagerly buys her
painting, "Girl with Swan,"

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.134
for 67,000 pounds, or
around 100,000 US dollars.

00:06:05.134 --> 00:06:06.330
(pensive music continues)

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:08.310
And once the painting is up for auction,

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.920
an expert, her name is Andrea Firmenich,

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:13.260
authenticates that the painting is real,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.960
giving credibility not
just to this one piece,

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:19.800
but to the whole collection
of Helena's grandfather.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.420
The newly discovered paintings

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.580
get the name Jager Collection,

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:25.350
and if Wolfgang and Helena happened to

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:26.850
sell more of these paintings,

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:29.463
they could make an
astonishing amount of money.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.343
The story was great.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.390
Works of art believed to
be destroyed by the Nazis

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:38.823
now discovered in a safe place.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.520
The believable background story

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.670
was one part of Wolfgang
and Helena's success.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:47.610
The other was that Wolfgang
came up with a clever approach

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.418
to creating his forgeries.

00:06:49.418 --> 00:06:50.400
(pensive music)

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:51.330
In the art world,

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.270
there are lots of famous
paintings that went missing,

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.630
but rather than recreating
famous lost works,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:01.170
Wolfgang paints entirely
new pieces of art,

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:05.100
paintings that never even
existed in the first place,

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.720
and he passes them off as
never-before-seen masterpieces.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.640
Wolfgang also avoids artists

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:14.280
that would attract the
absolute highest scrutiny,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.830
such as Picasso or Van Gogh,

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:18.840
and chooses instead to create paintings

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.240
from artists with massive catalogs

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.870
such as Heinrich Campendonk and Max Ernst.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.960
In the past, Wolfgang had
mostly sold his paintings

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.050
to flea markets and small-time art dealers

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.290
with some help from a friend,
Otto Schulte-Kellinghaus.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.330
But now, with his Jager Collection

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.510
being accepted as genuine
by the art establishment,

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:42.427
an entirely new world
has seemingly opened up.

00:07:42.427 --> 00:07:44.760
(dramatic music)

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.100
But just before he can start forging more,

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:49.863
the past is threatening
to catch up with him.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:53.550
Wolfgang hears about
the police investigation

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.951
related to his fake Molzahn paintings.

00:07:55.951 --> 00:07:57.210
(dramatic music continues)

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:58.440
And as mentioned before,

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.920
police almost had made
a connection to him.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:05.790
Immediately, Wolfgang and
Helena pack as much as they can

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:06.960
into a motor home,

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.756
and they move their family from
Germany to Southern France.

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:12.330
(dramatic music continues)

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:15.300
Though the timing of their
disappearance is suspicious,

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:18.390
the two to this day
heavily deny any allegation

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.370
that they were on the run.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.700
Rather, they insist their
daughter was chronically ill

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.923
and needed a change of environment.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.890
Once settled in France though,

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:30.240
Wolfgang isn't finished

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.758
supplying the art world
with his paintings.

00:08:32.758 --> 00:08:34.560
(bright music)

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:36.540
Anytime he finishes a new one,

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:38.970
either his wife Helena,
her sister Jeanette,

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:40.800
or Wolfgang's friend Otto,

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.130
will take the painting to
various auction houses.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.900
There, they face little questions

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:48.030
about the authenticity of the artworks,

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:50.790
likely because art
experts have now verified

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.280
many of the previous forgeries.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.080
Andrea Firmenich has included

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:59.040
several of Wolfgang's paintings
in her Campendonk Catalog.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.430
And Werner Spies, a leading
expert on Max Ernst,

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:05.673
has also verified seven forgeries as well.

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:10.590
The Jager Collection becomes
accepted in the art world.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:12.480
And with the backing of the experts,

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.140
Wolfgang and his wife go on
to begin amassing a fortune

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.660
as the price of their
paintings keeps rising.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.270
One of his fake Campendonk paintings

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:25.710
sells to actor Steve Martin for $860,000

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.840
and a forged Max Ernst painting

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.850
sells to a French magazine publisher

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:32.193
for a staggering $7 million.

00:09:33.561 --> 00:09:34.860
(pensive music)

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.860
The Beltracchis had put
together a well-working system

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:41.550
for creating the artworks,
marketing them to the art world,

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:43.980
and ultimately selling them.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:47.340
Now, if you are building a
real legitimate business,

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.430
you'd want all your
systems to work together,

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.040
and that is where Odoo comes in.

